  "strategy": "1.Approach and rationale:  \n   We design a step function that minimizes the maximum of its full self-convolution normalized by the square of the sum. The key insight is to use a sequence with highly oscillatory components to reduce autocorrelation peaks. Inspired by compressed sensing and sparse optimization, we apply gradient-projection methods in frequency space. This approach avoids local minima by introducing small phase perturbations and using an adaptive norm-reduction schedule.\n\n2.Mathematical specification:  \n   The objective is to minimize:  \n      *Upper bound = 2n * \\max(\\text{full conv}(a, a)) / (\\sum(a))^2*  \n\n   Representations:  \n      - Input domain: Coefficients a_i \u2265 0 and \u2211a_i \u2265 0.01.  \n      - Optimization domain: Use frequency components f_k, ensuring L2 recoverability.  \n\n   Formulate as:  \n      minimize \\parallel \\text{RFFT}(a) - b \\parallel_2^2 subject to \\sum_{k} |\\text{RFFT}(a)_k|^2 \\leq C_0, \\text{while lowering the norm of the time-domain representation}.\n\n   Use L-BFGS-B on frequency coefficients with an endpoint step-sizing mechanism.\n\n3.Implementation procedure:  \n\n   Steps:  \n\n   1. Start from initial sequence of positive real coefficients (non-normalized).  \n\n   2. Convert to frequency space using FFT.  \n\n   3. Find gradients using analytic frequency-domain calculations.  \n\n   4. Project gradients back to time domain ensuring non-negativity.  \n\n   5. Modify the current point via a regularized gradient step.  \n\n   6. Perform L-BFGS optimization with bound constraints.  \n\n   7. Schedule exploratory phase transitions every 100 optimizer steps.  \n\n   8. When constrained, transform via interpolation and frequency analysis.  \n\n   9. Always maintain a fallback candidate from previous optimization.  \n\n   10. Use the evaluate_sequence function to judge intermediate steps with a tolerance log factor.  \n\n4.Search choices and useful variations:  \n      - n_base = 100 (start with a base length)  \n      - n_init = 500 (longer initial trial length)  \n      - Optimization schedule uses adaptive step sizes:  \n           1. Initial severe projection step  \n           2. Then norm-reduction via Lagrange multipliers  \n           3. Use an intermediate number of dimensions (500) for peak evaluation  \n\n   The coder must implement resampling between time and frequency domains.\n\n5.Computational budget and robustness:  \n      - Dominant cost: 200 sample sizes \u2192 60 times faster than sparse representation.  \n      - Schedule: 20%, 80% weight allocation for decompression vs normalization.  \n      - Termination criteria:  \n           1. Max_iter = 10000,  \n\n6.Validation and return contract:  \n      - Check returned sequence via evaluate_sequence ensuring \u2211a_i \u2265 0.01.  \n      - Return list[floats] representing the best non-negative coefficient sequence.  \n      - If optimization stalls, return a fallback from parent length.",
